course: dictionary
lesson: 10193
title: Timidly: shy and nervous
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words show how a character feels, not just what they do. Timidly means in a shy, nervous, frightened way, without confidence. When a character speaks timidly, they are not sure of themselves, and they may be afraid of what will happen.
explain: Read this sentence from Alice: «When Alice timidly says "If you please, sir—", the Rabbit drops the gloves and fan and scurries away into the darkness.» Alice is not speaking boldly. She is nervous, so her voice is quiet and unsure. Ask yourself: how would she say it if she felt brave?
explain: The word timidly tells you about the character's feelings, so it helps you picture them. In The Wind in the Willows, Mole is timid at first in the Wild Wood, because the wood feels frightening to him. Notice the clues around a word, and they will often show you the feeling.
fact: Timidly means in a shy, nervous, frightened way.
fact: Timidly is an adverb. It tells you how someone does something, such as how they speak.
fact: The adjective timid means shy and nervous. Timidly is the same idea used to describe an action.
fact: «When Alice timidly says "If you please, sir—", the Rabbit drops the gloves and fan and scurries away.»
fact: Authors use words like timidly to help the reader feel a character's nervousness.
exercise: «When Alice timidly says "If you please, sir—"», what does timidly mean? | In a fast, cheerful way | In a loud, bold way | In a bored, lazy way | *In a shy, nervous way
exercise: Mole is timid at first in the Wild Wood. What does timid mean? | Very tall and strong | Happy and sure of himself | Angry and rude | *Shy and nervous
exercise: Why might an author write that a character speaks timidly? | To show the character is very hungry | To show the character is brave and loud | To show the character is bored | *To show the character feels shy and unsure
test: «She asked timidly if she could join the game.» How is she asking? | Angrily and loudly | *Shyly and nervously | Happily and proudly | Quickly and rudely
test: Which sentence shows someone acting timidly? | *The girl hid behind her mum and whispered her name | The dog ran straight at the stranger and barked | The team cheered and waved their flags | The boy shouted across the playground with a big grin
exam: «The new boy stepped timidly into the classroom.» Which word means the same as timidly? | Proudly | Noisily | *Nervously | Boldly
